[2026-03-04T21:18:43.337Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M5QJSJAULePmonMjHrLiBK","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay CLI Quick Start\n\n## Installation\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\n## Authentication\nGet your API key from https://app.replay.io settings and set the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Essential Commands\n\n**Recording:** Launch the Replay Browser and capture a session at a specified URL.\n```sh\nreplayio record https://myapp.com\n```\n\n**Listing:** View all local recordings with their IDs, timestamps, and upload status.\n```sh\nreplayio list\n```\n\n**Uploading:** Push recordings to the cloud after capturing them.\n```sh\nreplayio upload <recording-id>\n```\n\n**Management:** Remove unwanted recordings individually or in bulk.\n```sh\nreplayio remove [ids...]  # or use -a for all\n```\n\n**Login/Logout:** Authenticate to your Replay account or sign out (though API keys remain valid).\n\n## Key Points\n- Recording runs until you close the browser window\n- The CLI automatically checks for updates\n- Use `--json` flag with `list` for detailed output\n- Add `-h` to any command for additional help\n- Source maps can be uploaded separately using `upload-source-maps`"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c34180d3-cbd1-4463-bc62-c06f10740e19","uuid":"9318ff84-58cc-456c-bfee-b2682c194b11","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4854,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay CLI Quick Start\n\n## Installation\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\n## Authentication\nGet your API key from https://app.replay.io settings and set the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Essential Commands\n\n**Recording:** Launch the Replay Browser and capture a session at a specified URL.\n```sh\nreplayio record https://myapp.com\n```\n\n**Listing:** View all local recordings with their IDs, timestamps, and upload status.\n```sh\nreplayio list\n```\n\n**Uploading:** Push recordings to the cloud after capturing them.\n```sh\nreplayio upload <recording-id>\n```\n\n**Management:** Remove unwanted recordings individually or in bulk.\n```sh\nreplayio remove [ids...]  # or use -a for all\n```\n\n**Login/Logout:** Authenticate to your Replay account or sign out (though API keys remain valid).\n\n## Key Points\n- Recording runs until you close the browser window\n- The CLI automatically checks for updates\n- Use `--json` flag with `list` for detailed output\n- Add `-h` to any command for additional help\n- Source maps can be uploaded separately using `upload-source-maps`","durationMs":3171,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-04T21:18:43.659Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018P5MW4nraibUReF2QdU6m9","type":"tool_result","content":"# Key Instructions for Using Replay MCP Tools\n\n**Investigative Framework:**\nFollow a structured approach: formulate your question, use exploratory tools to identify relevant events, apply explanatory tools to understand causation, develop a hypothesis supported by data, and communicate your findings with evidence.\n\n**Core Principle:**\n\"The most important thing to remember when using Replay MCP to investigate a recording is to not jump to conclusions.\" Avoid speculation without verification.\n\n**Tool Categories:**\n\n*Exploratory Tools* discover what occurred and when:\n- Identify errors (uncaught exceptions, React rendering issues)\n- Review timelines (console messages, network requests, React renders)\n- Examine source code execution and hit counts\n\n*Explanatory Tools* clarify why events happened:\n- Trace dependencies between events\n- Get stack frames and control flow information\n- Inspect element details and component state\n\n**Best Practices:**\nWhen investigating JavaScript behavior, locate relevant code, verify execution, and use Logpoint to track expression values across multiple executions. For timing issues, identify the event point and use ControlDependency to examine prerequisite events and delays."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c34180d3-cbd1-4463-bc62-c06f10740e19","uuid":"a7a4ded4-1cf3-4c8a-a6ba-637ab6bed568","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Key Instructions for Using Replay MCP Tools\n\n**Investigative Framework:**\nFollow a structured approach: formulate your question, use exploratory tools to identify relevant events, apply explanatory tools to understand causation, develop a hypothesis supported by data, and communicate your findings with evidence.\n\n**Core Principle:**\n\"The most important thing to remember when using Replay MCP to investigate a recording is to not jump to conclusions.\" Avoid speculation without verification.\n\n**Tool Categories:**\n\n*Exploratory Tools* discover what occurred and when:\n- Identify errors (uncaught exceptions, React rendering issues)\n- Review timelines (console messages, network requests, React renders)\n- Examine source code execution and hit counts\n\n*Explanatory Tools* clarify why events happened:\n- Trace dependencies between events\n- Get stack frames and control flow information\n- Inspect element details and component state\n\n**Best Practices:**\nWhen investigating JavaScript behavior, locate relevant code, verify execution, and use Logpoint to track expression values across multiple executions. For timing issues, identify the event point and use ControlDependency to examine prerequisite events and delays.","durationMs":3151,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md"}}
